  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nAs the southeastern periphery of the mid/upper-level high pressure \nstarts filling in, this will allow upper-level disturbances to dive \nsouth across Texas this afternoon and evening. CAMs are in agreement \nthat this elevated convection will start in south-central Texas and \nmove south through the evening, approaching the Hwy 59 corridor. As \nthis precipitation will be elevated in nature, virga and evaporative \ncooling will allow for some gusty and erratic outflows with the \nstrongest storms, but this will be isolated. Where the CAMs do \nstruggle is how far south any of these showers/storms make it toward \nthe Middle Texas coast after 7 PM. For now, I have introduced some \n15-25% PoPs south of Hwy 59 after sunset, but the general trend is \nfor any shower activity to start becoming more isolated in nature \nand on a waning phase. \n\nAdditional shower activity will restart closer to the I-10 corridor \non Friday afternoon/evening, but that is where CAMs come into a bit \nmore of disagreement as to the initiation and coverage. There is \ngreater confidence in shower activity in the Victoria Crossroads \nfrom any remnant boundary and seabreeze activity. Saturday is more \nseabreeze-rainfall dominant, but nothing to atypical from your \npopcorn storm coverage/rainfall. \n\nIn the absence of any rainfall (or even worse, with any short-\nduration/light accumulation followed by copious sunshine), the \nHeatRisk brought by increased moisture will reach major to extreme \nlevels. Continue to practice heat safety and remember: hydrate, \nhydrate, hydrate!",
